CHAPTERS = {
    'Chapter 1: Introduction': '### 1.1 Background of the Study\n\nEvery customs officer who has worked with the State Warehouse, whether at a border post, an inland station or on an enforcement desk, will recognise the process this study is concerned with. Goods that cannot be entered on arrival end up in our custody under section 39 of the Customs and Excise Act \\[Chapter 23:02\\] and are logged in the RIH register while duty, penalties and warehouse rent remain outstanding. Others reach the warehouse through a Notice of Seizure under section 193, held while the person from whom the goods were taken works through the three-month window the law gives them to contest the seizure. Both processes are familiar to anyone who has handled the State Warehouse, and both have traditionally relied on the same tool: a paper register, filled in by hand, cross-checked from memory and by periodic physical stock-taking.\n\nASYCUDA World has become second nature for processing declarations, but it was never designed to answer the question that every officer who has handled the warehouse eventually has to ask: how long has this consignment actually been sitting here, and are we still within the law to hold it? That question is usually answered by walking to the shelf, finding the file and doing the arithmetic by hand. The sixty-day RIH clock and the ninety-day Notice of Seizure appeal window are therefore tracked much as they were a decade ago, manually, and only as often as someone remembers to check.\n\nThe consequence is one that most officers have seen play out. Goods quietly cross the sixty-day line without anyone noticing until the next physical audit catches them. By then, duty that should have been assessed weeks earlier is still uncollected, and a seizure whose appeal window lapsed months ago is still occupying warehouse space because nobody flagged it for disposal. This is not a failure of any individual officer’s diligence. It is what happens when a compliance deadline lives only in a paper file and nowhere else.\n\nThis research paper sets out the development of a Warehouse Management System built for the State Warehouse. It is designed to sit alongside ASYCUDA World, not to replace it, and to do the one thing the paper register cannot do: watch every RIH and Notice of Seizure entry continuously, flag it the moment it crosses its legal deadline, and carry it through a proper, auditable approval chain to its final disposition, whether that is release, forfeiture, destruction or e-auction.\n\n### 1.2 Statement of the Problem\n\nThe core difficulty is not that officers are careless with the paper register. It is that the register has no way of raising an alarm. A file sits in a drawer whether it is three days old or three months overdue, and the register cannot tell the difference on its own. Detection of an overstayed RIH or a lapsed Notice of Seizure appeal window therefore depends entirely on someone going and looking. In a busy station handling many consignments across several warehouses, that check happens only as often as staffing and workload allow, which in practice means periodic physical audits rather than continuous monitoring.\n\nThe downstream effect touches revenue directly. Duty, penalties and warehouse rent that should be assessed the moment a statutory period lapses often go uncollected until an audit catches the file. On the seizure side, goods whose appeal window has closed without challenge should move promptly toward disposal, yet without a system flagging that milestone they can sit in the warehouse indefinitely, tying up space and leaving the disposal decision undocumented. Supervisors and managers, meanwhile, have no consolidated view of how many entries are approaching their deadlines across the warehouses under their watch; that picture exists only in the memory of whoever has handled the most files.\n\nThe problem this study set out to solve, in language any officer would recognise, is this: how do we make the sixty-day RIH clock and the ninety-day Notice of Seizure clock ring on their own, without waiting for the next audit to notice, and how do we make sure that once a disposition decision is made it is signed off properly and recorded for good, the way a well-run warehouse file should be?\n\n### 1.3 Research Objectives\n\n**1.3.1 Main Objective**\n\nTo design, develop and pilot-test a digital Warehouse Management System that gives every RIH and Notice of Seizure entry a running clock against its correct statutory deadline, and carries it through a proper Officer–Supervisor–Manager sign-off chain to final disposition, operating alongside ASYCUDA World and not in place of it.\n\n**1.3.2 Specific Objectives**\n\n1.  To confirm, from the Act, the Regulations and ZIMRA’s own procedures, exactly how many days an RIH entry and a Notice of Seizure appeal are legally entitled to run, and to build those figures into the system as configurable rules rather than fixed code.\n\n2.  To build a capture screen that records everything an officer would normally write on an RIH or Notice of Seizure file (importer detail, goods description and vehicle detail where relevant) without missing a field the paper register would have caught.\n\n3.  To give officers a live, at-a-glance view of warehouse and vehicle-pound occupancy at their station, including the ability to mark a facility full.\n\n4.  To build a proper sign-off chain, in which the Officer requests, the Supervisor reviews and the Manager gives final approval, covering all four ways a detained item can leave the warehouse: release to its owner, forfeiture to the State, destruction, or sale by e-auction.\n\n5.  To capture the revenue side properly at the point of release: duty, additional duty and rent calculated automatically from the number of days the item actually spent in the warehouse.\n\n6.  To put the finished system in front of real officers and find out, through a structured questionnaire and statistical testing, whether it genuinely outperforms the paper register or merely looks as if it does.\n\n### 1.4 Research Questions\n\nThree questions sit behind this study, each explored through five questionnaire items put directly to pilot officers (see Chapter Three and Appendix A):\n\n- RQ1: What did the manual, paper-based process get wrong, in the experience of officers who used it every day?\n\n- RQ2: Does the Warehouse Management System genuinely fix those problems, or does it simply move them somewhere else?\n\n- RQ3: Is the system something an officer would actually want to use, and does it show any real benefit to compliance and revenue outcomes?\n\n### 1.5 Significance of the Study\n\nTo ZIMRA: a working tool that catches overstayed RIH goods and lapsed Notice of Seizure appeals before the next audit does, closing a revenue gap that has existed for as long as the paper register has been the only line of defence, at a fraction of the cost and disruption of modifying ASYCUDA World itself.\n\nTo officers and supervisors who work with the State Warehouse: a single screen that shows, at any moment, which files are approaching their deadline and which warehouses are near capacity, instead of that picture living only in the memory of whoever last checked the files.\n\nTo the research group: the chance to take a problem each of us has seen first-hand on rotation and turn it into something that works, sharpening our grounding in the Act, in systems design and in what it takes to build something an officer would trust enough to use daily.\n\nTo ZIMRA more broadly and to other revenue authorities facing the same paper-register problem: a documented, working example of what a lightweight digital layer can achieve without a full overhaul of the main customs platform.\n\n### 1.6 Scope and Delimitations\n\nThis study is confined to RIH and Notice of Seizure goods, piloted at Forbes Border Post. Removal in Bond and Removal in Transit were part of the original scope, but an early review found that ASYCUDA World already tracks those movements adequately. It was the State Warehouse and seizure side, still running on paper, that genuinely needed the work. The Warehouse Management System is built to sit alongside ASYCUDA World as a control layer, not to touch or replace it.\n\n### 1.7 Limitations\n\n- The pilot covers one station; a station with a different goods composition, staffing level or volume may see different results.\n\n- Questionnaire data collection was still under way as this chapter was written, so the sample available for the statistical analysis in Chapter Five may be modest.\n\n- Testing used a mix of live and simulated data, given the sensitivity of real warehouse records during a training placement; a full rollout would need a proper data-migration exercise first.\n\n### 1.8 Definition of Key Terms\n\n- RIH: goods conveyed to a State Warehouse under section 39 of the Act because entry was not made in time, held pending payment of duty, penalties and warehouse rent.\n\n- Notice of Seizure: the formal notice issued when goods are seized under section 193, opening a statutory window for the person concerned to contest the seizure before it becomes final.\n\n- Statutory period: the number of days the law allows before duty becomes payable or further action is required.\n\n- ASYCUDA World: the main system used to process customs declarations.\n\n- Disposition: what ultimately happens to a detained item: release to the owner, forfeiture to the State, destruction, or sale by e-auction.\n\n- Warehouse Management System: in this study, the digital system developed to monitor, approve and record the handling of RIH and Notice of Seizure goods in the State Warehouse.',
    'Chapter 2: Literature Review': '### 2.1 Introduction\n\nThis chapter does three things. First, it sets out the legal ground the study stands on. Second, it reviews the systems already in use around the world that touch the same problem: commercial warehouse management software, customs platforms such as ASYCUDA World, and enforcement systems for seized property. Third, it uses that review to establish the gap this study sets out to fill and to state plainly what the new Warehouse Management System improves on.\n\n### 2.2 Legal and Regulatory Framework\n\nEvery business rule in the Warehouse Management System traces back to a specific provision, not to a guess. Section 39(1)(b) of the Customs and Excise Act \\[Chapter 23:02\\] gives an importer ten days from importation to enter goods sitting in a transit shed, failing which they are treated as abandoned and moved to the State Warehouse. From there, section 39(2) gives a further period to enter the goods and settle duty, penalties and rent. That period is currently sixty days, not the three months many of us grew up hearing quoted on the floor. The sixty-day figure has been law since the Finance (No. 3) Act 10 of 2009 took effect on 8 January 2010, reducing it from the original three months. If the period lapses, section 39(3) allows the Commissioner to sell by public auction, with at least a month’s Gazette notice, or, for goods with no commercial value or that are dangerous or perishable, to sell out of hand, destroy or appropriate them.\n\nSection 193 governs seizures. Once a Notice of Seizure is issued, subsection (12) gives the person from whom the goods were taken three months to institute proceedings to recover them. If that window closes without a challenge, subsection (13) is unambiguous: the goods vest in the State, without compensation, and may be sold, destroyed or appropriated. It is worth stating plainly, because the two are easily blurred in daily practice, that the RIH clock and the Notice of Seizure clock are governed by different sections of the Act, run for different lengths of time and must never be treated as the same deadline. Getting this wrong in a manual file is an easy mistake. Getting it wrong in a system meant to apply the law automatically would be far worse, which is why the distinction was checked against the Act directly, rather than against institutional memory, before any part of the system was written.\n\nZIMRA’s own Consolidated CEP Procedures Summary (CEP0114, Treatment of Goods in Transit) supports the ten-day transit-shed figure and confirms that unresolved transit-shed goods become RIH, disposed of by rummage sale once the sixty-day period is spent. One finding is worth flagging to any officer reading this: while confirming these figures, we found that day-to-day practice at station level still sometimes cites the old three-month RIH figure, more than a decade after the law changed. That gap between what the Act says and what is repeated on the floor is a small but telling illustration of exactly the compliance risk this study is trying to close.\n\n### 2.3 What the Paper Register Gets Wrong\n\nNone of this will surprise an officer who has handled the State Warehouse, but it is worth setting down plainly. A paper register cannot flag anything on its own. It cannot tell you a file is overdue unless someone opens it and does the arithmetic. It cannot reconcile itself against what is physically on the warehouse floor. It has no memory of which officer handled which step of a disposal beyond whatever was written down at the time, and if that entry was skipped during a busy shift, that part of the trail is simply gone. These are not failures of any individual officer’s care. They are the built-in limits of a system that depends entirely on someone remembering to check.\n\n### 2.4 Review of Existing Systems\n\nThe idea of managing warehouse stock with software is not new, and neither is the use of computers to run customs. The question for this study is whether any system already in use covers the specific job of the State Warehouse: counting statutory days on detained goods, routing disposal decisions through an approval chain, and recording the revenue that results. The sections below review the main categories of system in turn.\n\n**2.4.1 Commercial Warehouse Management Systems**\n\nCommercial warehouse management systems are built to control the receipt, storage and shipment of goods for a business. A representative example is WMS-Trak, produced by CSA Software for freight forwarders (CSA Software, n.d.). It is described as a system for the efficient receipt, storage and shipment of customers’ products, and its listed features include inventory reconciliation, cycle counts down to individual stock-keeping unit, last-in-first-out and first-in-first-out tracking by client, bar-code scanning, automated customer invoicing, a customer portal for viewing inventory and requesting shipments, and electronic data interchange for purchase orders, receiving reports and shipping reports. It also integrates with the same vendor’s freight operations module.\n\nProducts of this kind answer real warehouse questions well: what is on the shelf, who owns it and when it needs to ship. They are designed around a commercial relationship in which the warehouse operator serves a client and bills for storage. The product description reviewed for this study does not mention bonded warehousing, customs clearance or duty-related functions. More fundamentally, the commercial model does not fit the State Warehouse. The goods there were not placed by a client who wants them back; they are held by the State under statute, the clock that matters is set by the Customs and Excise Act rather than by a contract, and the end of the process may be forfeiture, destruction or auction rather than shipment. Nor is there a place in such a product for the Officer–Supervisor–Manager approval chain that a customs disposal must pass through.\n\n**2.4.2 Customs Platforms: ASYCUDA and ASYCUDA World**\n\nThe Automated System for Customs Data (ASYCUDA) is developed by the United Nations Conference on Trade and Development (UNCTAD) and is described as a computerised customs management system covering most foreign trade procedures, including manifests, declarations, accounting, transit and suspense procedures (ASYCUDA, n.d.). It is configurable to the customs regimes, tariffs and legislation of each country, and ASYCUDA World is its fourth and latest generation, built on web-based technology. It is the platform on which declarations are processed in Zimbabwe, and officers will know it as the system of record for entries.\n\nASYCUDA’s strength is the declaration and the movement of goods under customs procedures. Its suspense procedures cover goods that move or are stored with duty suspended. What it is not described as doing is acting as the working register for goods that have fallen out of the declaration process altogether, such as goods that were never entered in time and now sit in the State Warehouse under section 39, or goods held under a Notice of Seizure. Those goods have no live declaration to carry their deadline. In practice the deadline therefore lives in a paper register beside the system, and this is the gap the officers’ daily experience points to.\n\n**2.4.3 Systems for Seized and Forfeited Property**\n\nThe closest published parallel to the seizure side of this study is the Seized Assets and Case Tracking System (SEACATS) used by United States Customs and Border Protection. The Department of Homeland Security describes it as the information system of record for the full lifecycle of enforcement incidents, tracking the physical inventory and disposition of seized assets, the administrative and criminal cases linked to them, case outcomes including fines, penalties and liquidated damages, and the financial records of collections tied to enforcement actions (U.S. Department of Homeland Security, 2017).\n\nSEACATS shows what a complete record looks like: seizure, case, disposition and money in one place. It also illustrates the point that national customs administrations treat seized goods as a records problem that deserves its own system. It is, however, a large federal system serving agencies with very different legal frameworks, and it is not a design that a single border station in Zimbabwe could adopt or afford. The lesson for this study is the scope of the record, not the scale of the technology.\n\n**2.4.4 The International Standard: The Revised Kyoto Convention**\n\nThe World Customs Organization’s Revised Kyoto Convention sets the international standard for customs warehousing in Specific Annex D, Chapter 1. It describes customs warehousing as storage of imported goods under customs control, without payment of import duties and taxes, and provides for public and private warehouses. It requires that, for non-perishable goods, the maximum storage period shall not be less than one year, and, importantly for this study, it states that national legislation shall specify the procedure to be followed where goods are not removed from the customs warehouse within the period laid down (World Customs Organization, n.d.).\n\nThat last requirement is the heart of the matter. The Convention leaves it to each country to write the overstay rule, and Zimbabwe has done so in sections 39 and 193 of the Act. It says nothing about how an administration should make sure the rule is actually applied on the day. That operational step is where the paper register falls short, and it is the step the Warehouse Management System is built to automate.\n\n### 2.5 Comparative Summary and Research Gap\n\nTable 2.1 sets the systems reviewed above against the requirements of the State Warehouse, as derived from the Act and from the daily experience described in Chapter One.\n\n| **Requirement**                                                            | **Commercial warehouse system** | **ASYCUDA World**                | **Seized-property system (SEACATS)** | **Warehouse Management System (this study)** |\n|----------------------------------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------|----------------------------------|--------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------|\n| **Counts statutory days against the correct section (s.39 vs s.193)**      | No                              | Not described for detained goods | Not applicable to Zimbabwean law     | Yes, configurable rules                      |\n| **Alerts when a deadline is reached**                                      | Not described                   | Not described for detained goods | Case-driven                          | Yes, automatically                           |\n| **Records both RIH and Notice of Seizure goods**                           | No                              | Partly (declarations only)       | Seizures only                        | Yes                                          |\n| **Officer–Supervisor–Manager approval of disposition**                     | No                              | Not described                    | Agency workflow                      | Yes, four pathways                           |\n| **Records duty, additional duty and rent, with rent counted by days held** | Invoicing for client storage    | Duty on declarations             | Fines and collections                | Yes, in USD and ZWG                          |\n| **Warehouse and vehicle-pound occupancy**                                  | Inventory only                  | Not described                    | Inventory only                       | Yes, with “full” flag                        |\n| **Fits a single station’s cost and skills**                                | Licensed commercial product     | National programme               | Federal programme                    | Yes, lightweight                             |\n| **Complements, rather than replaces, ASYCUDA World**                       | Not designed for it             | Is the platform                  | Not applicable                       | Yes                                          |\n\n*Table 2.1: Existing systems compared with the requirements of the State Warehouse. Entries marked “not described” reflect the sources reviewed and should not be read as proof that a feature is absent.*\n\n**2.5.1 The Gap**\n\nTaken together, the literature reviewed shows three things. Commercial warehouse systems manage stock for clients but have no concept of a statutory clock. Customs platforms such as ASYCUDA World process declarations and suspense procedures, but the goods in the State Warehouse are precisely those that have no live declaration to carry their deadline. Enforcement systems such as SEACATS prove that a complete seizure-to-disposal record is worth having, but they sit at a scale and under a legal framework that does not translate to a Zimbabwean border station. International standards require national law to set the overstay rule, and the Act does so, but no reviewed source describes a tool that applies that rule day by day to the goods on the floor.\n\nThe gap this study addresses is therefore specific: there is no lightweight system, designed around the Customs and Excise Act, that monitors RIH and Notice of Seizure goods against their separate statutory deadlines, routes their disposal through a documented approval chain and records the resulting revenue. The sources reviewed here are limited to publicly available material, and the group did not find a published evaluation of such a tool in the Zimbabwean setting. It should be said honestly that this absence of evidence is not proof that no such tool exists anywhere; it is the limit of what could be found.\n\n**2.5.2 What the Warehouse Management System Improves**\n\n- Deadlines that ring on their own: the sixty-day RIH clock and the ninety-day Notice of Seizure clock run automatically and are never confused, because each is a separate configurable rule tied to its section of the Act.\n\n- A complete disposal trail: every request passes from Officer to Supervisor to Manager and back to the Officer for finalisation, with each step recorded, in the way SEACATS shows a full record should look, but at the scale of one station.\n\n- Revenue captured at the point of release: duty, additional duty and rent are recorded as actually paid, in USD and ZWG, with rent counted from the first day the goods were held.\n\n- Visibility for supervisors and managers: one view of what is approaching its deadline and which warehouses are near capacity, in place of the memory of whoever handled the files.\n\n- Early attention for perishables: goods nearing expiry are flagged separately, in line with the provision for out-of-hand disposal of perishable goods.\n\n- A division of labour with ASYCUDA World: the new system does not process declarations and does not try to; it covers only the part of the process that ASYCUDA World is not described as covering.\n\n### 2.6 Conceptual Framework\n\nThe problem breaks down into four stages that map directly onto how an officer already thinks about a file. The first is capture: an officer records the RIH or Notice of Seizure entry, with everything the paper form would have asked for. The second is tracking: the system counts the days automatically against the correct legal threshold and against warehouse occupancy. The third is approval: a request moves through Officer, Supervisor and Manager, the same chain of authority a paper sign-off would follow. The fourth is finalisation: the Officer closes out the file with the actual payment, representative detail, destruction record or sale proceeds, the step that genuinely ends the file and locks in the audit trail. The design in Chapter Four follows this four-stage shape deliberately, so that the system mirrors how the work is already done and does not force officers to learn an unfamiliar process.\n\n### 2.7 Chapter Summary\n\nThis chapter has set out what the Act requires, namely sixty days for RIH and ninety for a Notice of Seizure appeal, and the weaknesses of the paper register that any officer would recognise. It has reviewed commercial warehouse systems, ASYCUDA World, the United States seized-asset system and the international customs warehousing standard, and has used that review to establish the gap: no lightweight, law-specific tool applies the statutory clock and approval chain to goods in the State Warehouse. Chapter Three sets out how this study went about building and testing a response.',
    'Chapter 3: Research Methodology': '### 3.1 Research Design\n\nThis study followed a design science research approach, which suits research that is judged in the end by whether the thing it built actually works. The cycle was straightforward: confirm the legal and procedural ground first, build and refine the system in stages, put it in front of real officers and evaluate it with a questionnaire and a statistical test, feeding what was learned back into the system as the work went on, rather than treating design and evaluation as separate, one-off steps.\n\n### 3.2 System Development Methodology\n\nThe system was built module by module, in the order an officer would naturally meet the work: login and role-based access first; then entry capture for RIH and Notice of Seizure, including vehicle detail; then warehouse and vehicle-pound occupancy tracking; then the four-pathway disposal workflow with its Officer–Supervisor–Manager sign-off; then revenue capture and reporting at the point of finalisation; then the audit trail and internal messaging; and finally the pilot questionnaire and results module. Each module was built, tested against realistic scenarios drawn from desk experience and deployed live before the next one was started, an incremental approach that allowed a wrong assumption to be caught early rather than discovered after the whole system was built.\n\nTwo modules were added after the first pilot feedback. The first produces the printable RIH and Notice of Seizure forms directly from the saved entry, so that the paper record and the system record can no longer drift apart. The second is a monthly **stocktake audit**: each month the Admin profile counts the goods physically present in every warehouse against the list the system expects, and the stocktake is scored out of 100 (items present divided by items expected plus extra items found). A score of 95 or above passes, allowing a 5% variance; anything lower is flagged as a potential fraud indicator, and the missing entries are flagged together with the responsible officer, supervisor and manager. Officers, supervisors and managers do not count, because they are the people being audited; they receive the results, monthly reports and their own rating.\n\n### 3.3 Population and Sampling\n\nThe officers we needed to hear from are those who touch this process: Officers who capture and process RIH and Notice of Seizure entries, Supervisors who give first-stage sign-off on disposal requests, and Managers who give final approval. We targeted roughly ten to fifteen officers for pilot participation, a number that reflects realistically who was available within the training period at a single station.\n\n### 3.4 Data Collection Instruments\n\nThe main instrument was a 15-item questionnaire, answered directly inside the system by logged-in pilot users on a scale from 1 (Strongly Disagree) to 5 (Strongly Agree). The fifteen items split into three groups of five, one for each research question: RQ1 on what the manual process got wrong, RQ2 on whether the digital system fixes it, and RQ3 on whether officers would want to use it. The full instrument is in Appendix A. Each officer has one response, which can be edited later. This avoided duplicate entries while still letting feedback evolve as officers became more familiar with the system over the pilot period.\n\n### 3.5 System Architecture and Development Tools\n\nThe system runs on Python with Streamlit as the web framework and PostgreSQL, hosted on Supabase through its session pooler connection for reliable access from cloud environments, as the database, with psycopg2 handling database access through a connection pool for performance. The code is kept in a private GitHub repository and deploys automatically to Streamlit Community Cloud on every update, so the pilot always runs the latest version without manual reinstallation. Passwords are hashed with bcrypt, and login sessions persist across a page refresh through a server-side session-token table, so an officer in the middle of capturing a file does not lose their place if the page reloads.\n\n### 3.6 Data Analysis Procedures\n\nQuestionnaire responses were analysed with descriptive statistics (mean score per item and per research-question group), a reliability check (Cronbach’s alpha) run separately for each five-item scale, and a paired-samples t-test comparing each officer’s RQ1 score with their own RQ2 score. This tests directly whether the digital system is seen as fixing the specific weaknesses officers named in the manual process. Significance was set at p \\< 0.05, the conventional threshold. The analysis runs live inside the system itself (using SciPy), so it can be re-run at any time as more responses come in, rather than being a one-off calculation done outside the tool.\n\n### 3.7 Ethical Considerations\n\nParticipation was voluntary and limited to ZIMRA staff using the system as part of their duties. No personal detail was collected beyond name, station and role, used solely for this research and for improving the system. Access throughout was controlled by role-based login, on a database instance restricted to authorised access.\n\n### 3.8 Chapter Summary\n\nThis chapter has set out the design science approach followed, how the system was built stage by stage, who it was tested with and how, the tools behind it and how the pilot data is analysed. Chapter Four turns to the system itself: how it is put together and how it works on the floor.',
    'References': 'ASYCUDA (n.d.) About ASYCUDA. Geneva: United Nations Conference on Trade and Development. Available at: https://asycuda.org/en/about/ (Accessed: 5 October 2026).\n\nCSA Software (n.d.) WMS-Trak: warehouse management for freight forwarders. Available at: https://csasoftware.com/freight-forwarding-software/wms-trak/ (Accessed: 5 October 2026).\n\nU.S. Department of Homeland Security (2017) Privacy Impact Assessment for the Seized Assets and Case Tracking System (SEACATS), DHS/CBP/PIA-040. Available at: https://www.dhs.gov/publication/dhscbppia-040-seized-assets-and-case-tracking-system (Accessed: 5 October 2026).\n\nWorld Customs Organization (n.d.) Revised Kyoto Convention, Specific Annex D, Chapter 1: Customs warehouses. Available at: https://www.wcoomd.org/en/topics/facilitation/instrument-and-tools/conventions/pf_revised_kyoto_conv/kyoto_new/spand.aspx (Accessed: 5 October 2026).\n\nRepublic of Zimbabwe (n.d.) Customs and Excise Act \\[Chapter 23:02\\], sections 39 and 193.\n\nRepublic of Zimbabwe (2010) Finance (No. 3) Act 10 of 2009.\n\nZimbabwe Revenue Authority (n.d.) Consolidated CEP Procedures Summary, CEP0114: Treatment of Goods in Transit.',
}

